  urldate = {2025-09-25},
  abstract = {Two major objectives are to be met by the mission-planning system, which was required for the GRACE mission. The first is to cast events resulting from orbit geometry into routine command generation, with as little of error prone operator interaction as possible. The second and more demanding requirement was to ensure uninterrupted science data collection, taking into account occasional dump failure. The project established the term "operational margin" for this requirement. The GRACE space segment consists of two satellites. The primary payload is an inter-satellite ranging system and precision accelerometers, as well as a GPS receiver. The latter opens possibilities for an additional experiment: determination of atmospheric data from RF occultation of GPS satellites. This occultation data constitute a considerable load on the on-board data storage. The solution must give room for the fact that one of the two DLR stations used for data collection has no uplink capability, thus restricting the reaction time needed for modifying a time-tag commanded dump sequence. It could be shown that data collection rates of several 10 Mbytes per day may be achieved, still allowing to compensate a dump failure within subsequent station contacts. Whereas time-scheduling problems we dealt with up to date could be formulated and solved using the terminology and methods of "constraint satisfaction" problems, this one could be transformed into an optimization problem, a "linear program" which could make use of standard algorithms of operational research.},
